<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-triple-integrals-cylindrical" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Triple Integrals in Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

<p>
Previously, we learned how to calculate triple integrals in rectangular
coordinates. Today, we will learn triple integrals in cylindrical
coordinates, which can be used to evaluate volumes of solids or to
integrate a function over a region in space. We will see that when
there is symmetry about an axis, it is more convenient to use
cylindrical coordinates to calculate a triple integral.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cylindrical-coordinates">
<title>Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

<p>
Cylindrical coordinates <m>(r,\theta,z)</m> are the combination of
polar coordinates <m>(r,\theta)</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane and the
<m>z</m>-coordinate of Cartesian coordinates; see
<xref ref="fig-cylindrical-point-tikz"/>. Thus the relations between
Cartesian and cylindrical coordinates are
</p>

<me>
x = r\cos\theta, \qquad y = r\sin\theta, \qquad z = z,
</me>

<me>
r^2 = x^2 + y^2, \qquad \tan\theta = \frac{y}{x}, \qquad z = z.
</me>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-point-tikz">
<caption>
A point <m>P(r,\theta,z)</m> in cylindrical coordinates:
<m>(r,\theta)</m> are the polar coordinates of its projection
<m>(r,\theta,0)</m> into the <m>xy</m>-plane, and <m>z</m> is its
usual height.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space. A point P is shown above the x y
plane. A segment of length r runs from the origin to the projection of
P in the x y plane, making an angle theta with the positive x axis,
and a vertical segment of length z connects the projection to P.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={125}{20},
      width=11cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=2.1, ymin=0, ymax=2.3, zmin=0, zmax=2.1,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % projection segment r
    \draw[oivermillion, line width=1.4pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1.3,1.35,0);
    % vertical segment z
    \draw[oiblue, line width=1.4pt]
      (axis cs:1.3,1.35,0) -- (axis cs:1.3,1.35,1.55);
    % angle arc theta
    \draw[oipurple, line width=1.0pt, -{Latex[length=1.6mm]}]
      (axis cs:0.5,0,0) .. controls (axis cs:0.5,0.26,0) ..
      (axis cs:0.35,0.36,0);
    % points
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.7pt, color=oiblue,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1.3,1.35,1.55)};
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.4pt, color=black,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1.3,1.35,0)};
    % labels
    \node[oiblue, anchor=south, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:1.3,1.35,1.62) {$P(r,\theta,z)$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.34,1.42,-0.1)
      {$(r,\theta,0)$};
    \node[oivermillion, anchor=north east, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:0.72,0.6,-0.02) {$r$};
    \node[oiblue, anchor=west, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:1.32,1.42,0.75) {$z$};
    \node[oipurple, anchor=west, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:0.58,0.2,0.08) {$\theta$};
    \node[anchor=south east, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.0,-0.05,0.02)
      {$O$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Before calculating triple integrals in cylindrical coordinates, we
need to figure out the volume element <m>dV</m> in this new set of
coordinates. Remember that the area element <m>dA</m> in polar
coordinates is <m>r\,dr\,d\theta</m>; stacking it through a height
<m>dz</m> gives the cylindrical volume element
</p>

<me>
dV = r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz,
</me>

<p>
which can be seen in <xref ref="fig-cylindrical-element-tikz"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-element-tikz">
<caption>
The cylindrical volume element: a polar rectangle of area
<m>r\,dr\,d\theta</m> (with sides <m>dr</m> and <m>r\,d\theta</m>)
extruded through a height <m>dz</m>, so
<m>dV = r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
A small curved block sitting at distance r from the z axis. Its base
is a polar rectangle with radial side d r and arc side r d theta, and
its height is d z. Dashed lines connect it to the z axis.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={125}{22},
      width=11cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=2.3, ymin=0, ymax=2.4, zmin=0, zmax=1.9,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % dashed rays to the element (base at r=1.5..1.9, theta=32..52 deg)
    \draw[black!55, dashed, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:{1.5*cos(32)},{1.5*sin(32)},0)
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:{1.5*cos(52)},{1.5*sin(52)},0)
      (axis cs:0,0,1.0) -- (axis cs:{1.5*cos(32)},{1.5*sin(32)},1.0)
      (axis cs:0,0,1.0) -- (axis cs:{1.5*cos(52)},{1.5*sin(52)},1.0);
    % ---- the element: bottom face ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.30]
      (axis cs:{1.5*cos(32)},{1.5*sin(32)},0)
      \foreach \a in {36,40,...,52} { -- (axis cs:{1.5*cos(\a)},{1.5*sin(\a)},0) }
      \foreach \a in {52,48,...,32} { -- (axis cs:{1.9*cos(\a)},{1.9*sin(\a)},0) }
      -- cycle;
    % outer wall
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.40]
      (axis cs:{1.9*cos(32)},{1.9*sin(32)},0)
      \foreach \a in {36,40,...,52} { -- (axis cs:{1.9*cos(\a)},{1.9*sin(\a)},0) }
      \foreach \a in {52,48,...,32} { -- (axis cs:{1.9*cos(\a)},{1.9*sin(\a)},1.0) }
      -- cycle;
    % side wall at theta = 32
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.22]
      (axis cs:{1.5*cos(32)},{1.5*sin(32)},0)
      -- (axis cs:{1.9*cos(32)},{1.9*sin(32)},0)
      -- (axis cs:{1.9*cos(32)},{1.9*sin(32)},1.0)
