    % top face
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.5]
      (axis cs:{1.5*cos(32)},{1.5*sin(32)},1.0)
      \foreach \a in {36,40,...,52} { -- (axis cs:{1.5*cos(\a)},{1.5*sin(\a)},1.0) }
      \foreach \a in {52,48,...,32} { -- (axis cs:{1.9*cos(\a)},{1.9*sin(\a)},1.0) }
      -- cycle;
    % edges (arcs)
    \addplot3[oiblue!70!black, line width=0.9pt, domain=32:52,
        samples=11, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1.5*cos(x)}, {1.5*sin(x)}, 0);
    \addplot3[oiblue!70!black, line width=0.9pt, domain=32:52,
        samples=11, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1.9*cos(x)}, {1.9*sin(x)}, 0);
    \addplot3[oiblue!70!black, line width=0.9pt, domain=32:52,
        samples=11, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1.5*cos(x)}, {1.5*sin(x)}, 1.0);
    \addplot3[oiblue!70!black, line width=0.9pt, domain=32:52,
        samples=11, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1.9*cos(x)}, {1.9*sin(x)}, 1.0);
    \draw[oiblue!70!black, line width=0.9pt]
      (axis cs:{1.5*cos(32)},{1.5*sin(32)},0) -- (axis cs:{1.5*cos(32)},{1.5*sin(32)},1.0)
      (axis cs:{1.9*cos(32)},{1.9*sin(32)},0) -- (axis cs:{1.9*cos(32)},{1.9*sin(32)},1.0)
      (axis cs:{1.5*cos(52)},{1.5*sin(52)},0) -- (axis cs:{1.5*cos(52)},{1.5*sin(52)},1.0)
      (axis cs:{1.9*cos(52)},{1.9*sin(52)},0) -- (axis cs:{1.9*cos(52)},{1.9*sin(52)},1.0);
    % labels
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:{1.72*cos(30)},{1.72*sin(30)},-0.06) {$dr$};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:{1.95*cos(42)},{1.95*sin(42)},0.5) {$dz$};
    \node[anchor=south west, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:{1.9*cos(54)},{1.9*sin(54)},-0.28) {$r\,d\theta$};
    \node[anchor=north east, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:{0.75*cos(42)},{0.75*sin(42)},-0.02) {$r$};
    \node[anchor=south, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:0.35,0.28,0.02) {$d\theta$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Now suppose our goal is to integrate over a region <m>E</m> described
as
</p>

<me>
E = \left\{(x,y,z)\ \middle|\ (x,y)\in D,\
u_1(x,y) \le z \le u_2(x,y)\right\},
</me>

<p>
where the region <m>D</m> is known in polar coordinates:
</p>

<me>
D = \left\{(r,\theta)\ \middle|\ \alpha\le\theta\le\beta,\
h_1(\theta) \le r \le h_2(\theta)\right\}.
</me>

<p>
Then the integral
<m>\iiint_E f\,dV
= \iint_D\big[\int_{u_1}^{u_2} f\,dz\big]dA</m> can be converted to
cylindrical coordinates:
</p>

<me>
\iiint_E f(x,y,z)\,dV
= \int_{\alpha}^{\beta}\!\!\int_{h_1(\theta)}^{h_2(\theta)}
\!\!\int_{u_1(r\cos\theta,\,r\sin\theta)}^{u_2(r\cos\theta,\,r\sin\theta)}
f(r\cos\theta,\ r\sin\theta,\ z)\ r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta.
</me>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cylindrical-mass-example">
<title>The Mass of a Solid With Axial Symmetry</title>

<example xml:id="example-cylindrical-mass">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
A solid <m>E</m> lies within the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, below
the plane <m>z = 4</m>, and above the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m>. Given that the density of the solid at any
point is proportional to its distance from the axis of the cylinder,
find the mass of the solid.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
Let us begin by plotting the region of integration
(<xref ref="fig-cylindrical-solid-tikz"/>). Note that the equation of
the cylinder in cylindrical coordinates is <m>r = 1</m>, and the
equation of the paraboloid is
</p>

<me>
z = 1 - x^2 - y^2
= 1 - \left(r^2\cos^2\theta + r^2\sin^2\theta\right) = 1 - r^2.
</me>

<p>
Therefore, the limits of integration can be written as
</p>

<me>
E = \left\{(r,\theta,z)\ \middle|\ 0\le\theta\le 2\pi,\
0 \le r \le 1,\ 1 - r^2 \le z \le 4\right\},
</me>

<p>
since the projection into the <m>xy</m>-plane is a circle of radius
<m>1</m> and the height of the solid is bounded between the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - r^2</m> and <m>z = 4</m>. Since we know that the density
<m>\delta(x,y,z)</m> of the solid is proportional to the distance from
the axis of the cylinder, we have
</p>

<me>
\delta(x,y,z) = K\sqrt{x^2 + y^2},
</me>

<p>
and since <m>r = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, in cylindrical coordinates
<m>\delta(r,\theta,z) = Kr</m>. Thus, we can compute the mass of the
solid as follows:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
m = \iiint_E K\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dV
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1\!\!\int_{1-r^2}^{4}
(Kr)\,r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1
Kr^2\left[4 - \left(1 - r^2\right)\right]dr\,d\theta
= K\int_0^{2\pi}\!d\theta\int_0^1\left(3r^2 + r^4\right)dr
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= 2\pi K\left[r^3 + \frac{r^5}{5}\right]_0^1
= \frac{12\pi K}{5}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-solid-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of <xref ref="example-cylindrical-mass"/>: inside the
cylinder <m>r = 1</m>, above the paraboloid <m>z = 1 - r^2</m>, and
below the plane <m>z = 4</m>. Its mass, with density
<m>\delta = Kr</m>, is <m>\frac{12\pi K}{5}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a vertical circular cylinder of
radius one about the z axis, capped by the plane z equals four on top.
Inside the bottom of the cylinder, the paraboloid z equals one minus r
squared forms a dome shaped floor that touches the point zero zero one
on the z axis and meets the cylinder wall at the x y plane.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={125}{16},
      width=10.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-1.4, xmax=1.7, ymin=-1.4, ymax=1.8, zmin=0, zmax=4.9,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- paraboloid floor z = 1 - r^2 ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=black!40,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softp}{color=(oivermillion!15!white) color=(oivermillion!45!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:360, samples=31,
              y domain=0:1, samples y=9,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {1 - y^2});
    % ---- cylinder wall ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              colormap={softc}{color=(oiblue!8!white) color=(oiblue!32!white)},
              opacity=0.32, fill opacity=0.32,
              domain=0:360, samples=49,
              y domain=0:1, samples y=2,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {y*4});
    % ---- top cap z = 4 ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.30]
      (axis cs:1,0,4)
      \foreach \a in {10,20,...,350} { -- (axis cs:{cos(\a)},{sin(\a)},4) }
      -- cycle;
    \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.2pt, domain=0:360, samples=73,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, 4);
    % base circle
    \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.0pt, dashed, domain=0:360,
        samples=73, samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, 0);
    % axis dashed
    \draw[black!55, dashed, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,1) -- (axis cs:0,0,4);
    % points and labels
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.5pt, color=black,
        forget plot] coordinates {(0,0,4) (0,0,1) (1,0,0)};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.07,0.07,4.18)
      {$(0,0,4)$};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.1,0.12,1.18)
      {$(0,0,1)$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.12,0,-0.12)
      {$(1,0,0)$};
    \node[font=\small, anchor=east] at (axis cs:-1.05,-0.5,3.5)
      {$z = 4$};
    \node[oivermillion, font=\small, anchor=west]
      at (axis cs:0.55,0.9,0.35) {$z = 1 - r^2$};
    \node[oiblue, font=\small, anchor=west]
      at (axis cs:0.1,1.15,2.6) {$r = 1$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The way a column of the solid runs from the paraboloid floor up to the
plane <m>z = 4</m>, and how it sweeps around the axis, is animated in
<xref ref="fig-cylindrical-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cylindrical-video">
<caption>
Animation of the cylindrical setup for
<xref ref="example-cylindrical-mass"/>. The solid is assembled from
the cylinder <m>r = 1</m>, the plane <m>z = 4</m>, and the paraboloid
floor <m>z = 1 - r^2</m>; a column at <m>(r,\theta)</m> runs from
<m>z = 1-r^2</m> up to <m>z = 4</m>, and sweeps through the solid as
<m>\theta</m> goes around the axis, illustrating
<m>dV = r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-cylindrical-column"
       source="videos/cylindrical_column_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
